#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147372 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "18371fa9-696a-5a3a-b7e6-400f14d2fe62" :question/math-academy-id "e-6724" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?\n\nI. $y''+4y' - y = 0$\n\nII. $y''+ 4 = 0$\n\nIII. $y''+y'+4y = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt would require $\\omega^2=-1,$ which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0.$\n\n- Equation II does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nbecause it has a constant term, $4.$\n\n- Equation III takes the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 2\n$$\n\nand $b = 1.$ Therefore, equation III represents damped harmonic motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147373 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/id #uuid "365ec1d4-0301-5dfe-a731-35f12ea4d3a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6725" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n\nII. $y'' +2y'+ y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nIII. $y'' +4y'+3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} = 0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} < 0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda + 2 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={1}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =-7<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to light damping. Therefore, statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +2y'+y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +2\\lambda + 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to critical damping. Therefore, statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +4y'+3 y$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +4\\lambda + 3 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =4>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to heavy damping. Therefore, statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/id #uuid "0458947c-70c8-5f88-9e52-68c8950afd7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6726" :question/problem "A particle of mass $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10\\, \\textrm{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y .\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n5 y'' =-10y' -15 y .\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+15y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+3y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/id #uuid "c93a18f3-6d09-5d88-8a90-434fc08d3874" :question/math-academy-id "e-6727" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 2y' + 5y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the period of the damped oscillation, we need to find the solution and then find the period of the sinusoidal terms in the solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we first find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 2\\lambda + 5 = 0\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c =e^{-t}\\left(A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $\\Omega = 2\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi \\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f409a79-4fae-5439-b230-be0345bd9ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334266} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb1f168-10a4-5c91-9fbb-3351c0c7f3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334629} {:answer/id #uuid "c150a3e3-95b7-5e28-ba0f-c354f8ba3c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334664} {:answer/id #uuid "d1255f31-c36d-50a7-9c97-00b4ea4c3c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334816} {:answer/id #uuid "e92dd86f-7f2c-58f4-92d5-514545e54ff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bcb1f168-10a4-5c91-9fbb-3351c0c7f3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334629} :answer-field/id #uuid "c408ffb6-3f92-5bb9-b345-bdde76a8b695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15584f20-fb01-5c6f-87f6-787ded6f6315" :question/math-academy-id "q-64547" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+10y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=3\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23192ffb-c5ff-5772-abd5-beb8dbc77391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9y={e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432799} {:answer/id #uuid "ef917fb3-8de5-5591-8539-94e826aa4bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+25y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432817} {:answer/id #uuid "f363187b-6c07-55e7-8bd6-d9fde1f982ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432818} {:answer/id #uuid "f4937738-4f40-5a50-b1b9-b581e2efc232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+16y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432819} {:answer/id #uuid "fa80ea3b-48bf-5ceb-ae4d-04796c4fd4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa80ea3b-48bf-5ceb-ae4d-04796c4fd4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432821} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9a2aa2-4314-5e20-994f-9b028b117d14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76c1e10b-ff92-55c3-9f48-cde089c3ec98" :question/math-academy-id "q-64587" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35552ebe-c13c-5001-b3d4-36eb88b5f7b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+15y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333480} {:answer/id #uuid "70a7c389-0770-544e-944a-627cac1fff45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{5}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334016} {:answer/id #uuid "786bf5ca-3c94-5cb0-b6a4-6d9461bf2323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+5y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334070} {:answer/id #uuid "88acabd5-be5a-5529-8cff-1b08362ddfe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+5y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334201} {:answer/id #uuid "f9780d48-04d9-5b5e-905a-b16186bf6f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{5}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88acabd5-be5a-5529-8cff-1b08362ddfe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+5y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334201} :answer-field/id #uuid "685ab961-babf-537b-ada0-89777f442836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f786c29-e425-58af-9063-5fe1b456dd96" :question/math-academy-id "q-64591" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=9\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-9{y}^{′}-15y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6f5987-69f9-573e-9ddc-cfd86f08493d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+1.5{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333083} {:answer/id #uuid "1232e95d-98d6-52ff-84be-cc91cbb071ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333151} {:answer/id #uuid "4f13f201-87e9-5c80-aed6-84593e1f6d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333714} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9147b1-00f8-5d49-b36f-4e089df4185c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333845} {:answer/id #uuid "9218b3d9-3d65-5f13-834a-3ae1cf6242aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+1.5{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9218b3d9-3d65-5f13-834a-3ae1cf6242aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+1.5{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334291} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f24b33b-282a-5040-aaa8-ece31c744d70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1e89040-067b-5e6c-a0cb-d3d762298784" :question/math-academy-id "q-64592" :question/problem "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c=6\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-6{y}^{′}-8y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+1.5{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f723e96-857d-5e68-ac49-6ed9b317af9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333590} {:answer/id #uuid "418a04f9-3913-537d-a217-e8e531b9d278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333604} {:answer/id #uuid "4db942f9-e54f-5e6d-9f98-93aedc31a805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333703} {:answer/id #uuid "6733cd52-d910-536c-80de-583199f21e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333932} {:answer/id #uuid "91f9073d-47c7-5544-9a15-75ec93d203e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+18y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "418a04f9-3913-537d-a217-e8e531b9d278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333604} :answer-field/id #uuid "439241dd-f64f-51cf-892f-aeb3b80f08b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e5d3139-8000-5f12-824c-92daaf2b4fdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-64593" :question/problem "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's speed, and has a damping coefficient $c=10\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-10{y}^{′}-18y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "120a7cf6-5730-5b28-b0d8-c1e791963e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333150} {:answer/id #uuid "1b63744f-e7f3-5e9f-9ee0-eeb45a9ec5cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333238} {:answer/id #uuid "3d11d708-2d68-5095-adaa-5073f6069713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333564} {:answer/id #uuid "495a738b-9682-5553-9eb1-4c600690075b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333665} {:answer/id #uuid "97528c2d-847c-599f-9b47-dcdfbe437a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d11d708-2d68-5095-adaa-5073f6069713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333564} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab33f5c8-2a16-511d-b5e0-f528bae70ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c1c35ed-7049-53ec-934c-7b92d30ad741" :question/math-academy-id "q-64616" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping." :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(6) & =1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +2=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =8>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "398637b9-9de8-5603-8107-020a5725e120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333526} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6f2b94-98ce-59eb-9977-cd986817c2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333844} {:answer/id #uuid "75af940b-97d4-5a46-8776-33f173701d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334052} {:answer/id #uuid "d124aebd-c863-5507-aa4c-186618654ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334815} {:answer/id #uuid "f57a5947-95ad-5807-ba8c-d3f854bb8495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75af940b-97d4-5a46-8776-33f173701d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334052} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d4eec8b-2a2a-5ef1-a4bf-644458b1a312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5a95c4f-6f0b-5fe1-b32c-ae9f75fde633" :question/math-academy-id "q-64617" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping.\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping." :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +7=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d7c71da-e88e-5ef7-9992-57e3285741a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392872} {:answer/id #uuid "87ab7734-6709-5366-9804-65686fe38f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392873} {:answer/id #uuid "bc219892-8db2-59ec-8f0e-74228c0dcdd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392874} {:answer/id #uuid "90aaa559-d83e-58cb-8b19-dca2ca99dcd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392875} {:answer/id #uuid "af845d3e-6ea7-51b2-9edf-f53410cbddd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d7c71da-e88e-5ef7-9992-57e3285741a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392872} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29649ee-a16d-56d9-80ba-8cd64f7d549a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e801805-48d7-5f7f-a2eb-42e2eeee70b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64623" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+52y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +52=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +52 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+52 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-36 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=6\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "908ef03e-2769-52ca-bdea-efd9e7c58c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432808} {:answer/id #uuid "d2951689-73d1-537c-aae4-36d5122518f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432811} {:answer/id #uuid "dca0d751-a78d-50e2-8aa2-db827b1d277c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432813} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e1684d-8e00-5782-9e5e-23e3c0e032c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432815} {:answer/id #uuid "e631dceb-185d-59ac-ba82-82d589019dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2951689-73d1-537c-aae4-36d5122518f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432811} :answer-field/id #uuid "7015251f-935c-5f1f-96ba-22875987af52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25b5e38d-2fa5-5f4d-8952-608bc6de5c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-64624" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+13y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +13 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+13 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=2\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{2}=\\pi \\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad85367-37ea-500f-8405-f9464bf4f773" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7dba1f-5e33-52d7-813e-cd6fb16685c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345588} {:answer/id #uuid "1e99a210-2fa5-5f85-a254-2108d135099f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345620} {:answer/id #uuid "70ae2387-f0f0-5f3d-afd0-f32f68d6d09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346342} {:answer/id #uuid "772b4402-4fb6-53f6-a6a7-20c33d9c7207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-6y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346396} {:answer/id #uuid "8de02028-f4c8-5dfd-8cb7-0c23d3d29c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70ae2387-f0f0-5f3d-afd0-f32f68d6d09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+9y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346342} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b885d99-602b-5337-a40b-a0aa9f8904e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1ea8a40-7590-52ee-8cd6-f0110c352d80" :question/math-academy-id "q-79498" :question/problem "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+9y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa9cb0a8-557e-5b0b-9753-10f618e7048e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393942} {:answer/id #uuid "97d1921f-94da-5b6b-876f-e6446a353e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393943} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ce6670-2040-58d6-a375-b175a3d11db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393944} {:answer/id #uuid "c1392899-998d-54f1-858c-eece8ca396f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393945} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbb88df-1a01-5758-84f9-1948b1c83343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4ce6670-2040-58d6-a375-b175a3d11db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393944} :answer-field/id #uuid "519ee466-f0d0-576d-b575-b46d45151fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5708124f-9743-5ab7-8b57-9c32436ebfac" :question/math-academy-id "q-79571" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +41=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+41 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=5\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29809ee8-292b-5c9c-913b-0bb723fc7ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355010} {:answer/id #uuid "3ac74971-63d8-5b23-8fcf-97c3ece1fcb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355194} {:answer/id #uuid "3c628be3-3714-5241-9233-0637686d6ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355205} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ddee6f-5ab0-5a93-9234-09c063951125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356167} {:answer/id #uuid "b501d685-a302-5692-a5b9-3dc90442ee5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c628be3-3714-5241-9233-0637686d6ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355205} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd55c365-f91e-5a88-be8e-7db91b2b6861" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6276abb-11ab-5f2c-8db5-0bce52daa784" :question/math-academy-id "q-94311" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +7=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a71eb77-c12c-5cec-bf11-0e5015bd9045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355330} {:answer/id #uuid "58d37840-4038-5001-bec7-7d59f6b97472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355452} {:answer/id #uuid "72b3d522-e125-5325-b715-b212bf875da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355674} {:answer/id #uuid "76b2efa3-bff5-54ff-b62d-1e854ea6e203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355705} {:answer/id #uuid "f08bc086-5017-5952-b23a-dc70bf9be510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76b2efa3-bff5-54ff-b62d-1e854ea6e203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355705} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df2e1f8-1c96-5da0-8938-5d076e74e024" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2840cb37-803e-5355-a4ea-a188990899c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94313" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-4(1)(2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =13>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+14y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +14=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(14) & =-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a699d4d-481b-5164-8a94-fe6421c0ee70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432800} {:answer/id #uuid "31ffbbd1-678b-5808-8fca-cdd7644b7978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432802} {:answer/id #uuid "635d6934-78cc-51ab-a251-c7a8c8ed0bb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432804} {:answer/id #uuid "902fe176-8794-5e7f-a141-85bc2b1d225d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432807} {:answer/id #uuid "d225cf09-2533-5d78-b568-46029a1627a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "902fe176-8794-5e7f-a141-85bc2b1d225d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432807} :answer-field/id #uuid "578e80cb-b054-52ce-97be-509dd668d752" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d285631-ec24-50a4-ab3c-c0601632cbad" :question/math-academy-id "q-94314" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping\n\nII. ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\nIII. ${y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+36y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+4y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(4) & =48>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =4>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+36y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={12}^{2}-4(1)(36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f217b91-26db-54d6-8126-a7bfbc2461c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+6y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432801} {:answer/id #uuid "7e16aa15-0013-5d80-9600-471158f1dfa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+18y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432805} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f560a0-d4b7-5674-91a8-c59f28f39b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432809} {:answer/id #uuid "d8aaa50e-b1c0-5da1-be48-5bf477ec6ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+3\\sqrt{2}\\,y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432812} {:answer/id #uuid "f64c5d26-0b90-5645-b4d8-a2cc1ce6a4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f217b91-26db-54d6-8126-a7bfbc2461c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+6y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432801} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bf5f75c-ce4c-503a-9ca3-208934e1466a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db14497d-6b9b-508e-a43a-c6705f9b9e88" :question/math-academy-id "q-94320" :question/problem "A particle of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=18\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=6\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n3{y}^{″}=-6{y}^{′}-18y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+6y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147446 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dfa8f9b2-1628-5f9f-8f0e-0fd6c01c70d4" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Damped Harmonic Motion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "360c67dc-8ce5-5b6a-9fab-195ebaf50ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355154} {:answer/id #uuid "643a5d89-9db2-5e2f-a8a3-25918b1333cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355559} {:answer/id #uuid "79a78510-f1b1-5a86-af52-b831c596b0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{10}\\,y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355729} {:answer/id #uuid "95c2f663-f11a-5343-94b3-78d8c4600258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355981} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ec6a8e-fae7-500f-aaec-78b56f43c7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}^{′}+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "643a5d89-9db2-5e2f-a8a3-25918b1333cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{2}y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355559} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e732017-d688-56ef-8e14-7c1c3c06bdb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9da3526-636b-5179-9f25-351d09bfb11d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94321" :question/problem "A particle of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=8\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n4{y}^{″}=-8{y}^{′}-10y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+\\frac{5}{2}y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a64312-22d5-57ed-b83b-254aff2ca870" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Damped Oscillation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d92886-a0b6-558c-8d1d-01e1a39fc792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432797} {:answer/id #uuid "12588678-dc2f-56ca-b7ad-d201ff8cd771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432798} {:answer/id #uuid "61f58f09-3464-5d14-a7fc-6b2c92612ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432803} {:answer/id #uuid "8db5b483-0165-56b2-a792-56ccfc28ac64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432806} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b28ba7-4b44-5c62-bebc-3289f103e700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b28ba7-4b44-5c62-bebc-3289f103e700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432814} :answer-field/id #uuid "8174c21b-eafc-56be-ad59-cefe6b8b533d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c4b5efd-8648-5dd6-b5f3-7b3ca42bb25b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94360" :question/problem "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+25y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" :question/worked-solution "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-3t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=4\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$"}]}
